<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net">
  <diagram name="Iterator Pattern" id="iterator">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1434" dy="844" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1400">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        
        <!-- Title -->
        <mxCell id="title" value="ITERATOR DESIGN PATTERN - Sequential Access Without Exposing Structure" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=20;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- DEFINITION -->
        <mxCell id="def_title" value="PURPOSE: Access collection elements sequentially WITHOUT exposing underlying representation" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="70" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- JAVA COLLECTIONS EXAMPLE -->
        <mxCell id="java_title" value="REAL EXAMPLE: Java Collections (THE biggest client of Iterator Pattern!)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="120" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="java_example" value="PROBLEM:&#xa;  ArrayList stores data in ARRAY (index-based access)&#xa;  LinkedList stores data in LINKED NODES (pointer-based access)&#xa;  HashSet stores data in HASH TABLE (no order, hash-based)&#xa;  TreeSet stores data in RED-BLACK TREE (sorted order)&#xa;  &#xa;  QUESTION: How to iterate ALL without knowing internal structure?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;WITHOUT ITERATOR PATTERN:&#xa;  // Client MUST know internal structure!&#xa;  ArrayList list = new ArrayList();&#xa;  for (int i = 0; i &lt; list.size(); i++) {  // Array-based access&#xa;    Object obj = list.get(i);&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  LinkedList linkedList = new LinkedList();&#xa;  Node current = linkedList.head;  // ✗ Client knows 'head', 'next'!&#xa;  while (current != null) {&#xa;    Object obj = current.data;&#xa;    current = current.next;&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  ✗ Different iteration logic for each collection!&#xa;  ✗ Client tightly coupled to internal structure!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;WITH ITERATOR PATTERN:&#xa;  // Client uses SAME code for ALL collections!&#xa;  Collection collection = new LinkedHashSet();  // Or ArrayList, LinkedList, etc.&#xa;  collection.add('A');&#xa;  collection.add('B');&#xa;  collection.add('C');&#xa;  &#xa;  Iterator iterator = collection.iterator();  // Get iterator&#xa;  while (iterator.hasNext()) {  // SAME for all!&#xa;    Object value = iterator.next();&#xa;    System.out.println(value);&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  ✓ SAME code works for ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, TreeSet!&#xa;  ✓ Client doesn't know: array, linked nodes, hash table, tree&#xa;  ✓ Collection hides internal structure&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;HOW IT WORKS:&#xa;  1. Collection exposes iterator() method&#xa;  2. Each collection returns APPROPRIATE iterator:&#xa;     - ArrayList → ArrayListIterator (index-based)&#xa;     - LinkedList → LinkedListIterator (pointer-based)&#xa;     - HashSet → HashSetIterator (hash-based)&#xa;  3. All iterators implement SAME interface (hasNext, next)&#xa;  4. Client uses interface — doesn't care about implementation!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;THIS IS ITERATOR PATTERN!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="160" width="1520" height="620" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- UML COMPONENTS -->
        <mxCell id="uml_title" value="PATTERN COMPONENTS" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="820" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="components" value="1. ITERATOR INTERFACE (defines traversal methods)&#xa;   interface Iterator {&#xa;     boolean hasNext();  // Are there more elements?&#xa;     Object next();      // Get next element &amp; advance&#xa;   }&#xa;&#xa;2. CONCRETE ITERATOR (implements traversal for specific collection)&#xa;   class BookIterator implements Iterator {&#xa;     private List&lt;Book&gt; books;  // Collection to iterate&#xa;     private int index = 0;     // Current position&#xa;     &#xa;     public boolean hasNext() {&#xa;       return index &lt; books.size();  // Check if more elements&#xa;     }&#xa;     &#xa;     public Object next() {&#xa;       Book book = books.get(index);  // Get current&#xa;       index++;                        // Move forward&#xa;       return book;&#xa;     }&#xa;   }&#xa;&#xa;3. AGGREGATE INTERFACE (defines iterator creation method)&#xa;   interface Aggregate {&#xa;     Iterator createIterator();  // Factory method&#xa;   }&#xa;&#xa;4. CONCRETE AGGREGATE (implements collection &amp; iterator creation)&#xa;   class Library implements Aggregate {&#xa;     private List&lt;Book&gt; books;  // Internal storage&#xa;     &#xa;     public Library(List&lt;Book&gt; books) {&#xa;       this.books = books;&#xa;     }&#xa;     &#xa;     public Iterator createIterator() {&#xa;       return new BookIterator(books);  // Return appropriate iterator&#xa;     }&#xa;   }&#xa;&#xa;5. CLIENT (uses iterator, doesn't know internal structure)&#xa;   class Client {&#xa;     public static void main(String[] args) {&#xa;       List&lt;Book&gt; bookList = Arrays.asList(&#xa;         new Book('Design Patterns'),&#xa;         new Book('Clean Code'),&#xa;         new Book('Refactoring')&#xa;       );&#xa;       &#xa;       Library library = new Library(bookList);&#xa;       Iterator iterator = library.createIterator();  // Get iterator&#xa;       &#xa;       while (iterator.hasNext()) {  // Standard traversal&#xa;         Book book = (Book) iterator.next();&#xa;         System.out.println(book.getName());&#xa;       }&#xa;     }&#xa;   }&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;KEY RELATIONSHIPS:&#xa;  ✓ Aggregate (Library) HAS-A collection (books)&#xa;  ✓ Aggregate creates appropriate Iterator (BookIterator)&#xa;  ✓ Iterator HAS-A reference to collection (for traversal)&#xa;  ✓ Client uses Iterator interface (doesn't know BookIterator)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;MAGIC:&#xa;  Change Library from List to Array?&#xa;    → Only Library &amp; BookIterator change&#xa;    → Client code UNCHANGED!&#xa;  &#xa;  Want different iteration order (reverse)?&#xa;    → Create ReverseBookIterator&#xa;    → Client code UNCHANGED!&#xa;" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=9;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="860" width="1520" height="860" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- INTERVIEW QUESTIONS -->
        <mxCell id="q_title" value="INTERVIEW QUESTIONS" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1760" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="questions" value="Q1: Why Java Collections use Iterator instead of for-loop with get(index)?&#xa;A1: NOT all collections support index-based access! LinkedList has no get(index) — O(n) traversal per get! HashSet has NO order — no concept of index. Iterator abstracts traversal mechanism. Works for: arrays (index), linked lists (pointers), trees (in-order), hash tables (buckets). Uniform interface across all data structures. Client doesn't need to know 'how to iterate', just 'hasNext() and next()'.&#xa;&#xa;Q2: Iterator Pattern vs forEach / enhanced for-loop in Java?&#xa;A2: Enhanced for-loop USES Iterator internally! for (Book book : library) compiles to: Iterator iter = library.iterator(); while (iter.hasNext()) { Book book = iter.next(); ... }. Syntactic sugar over Iterator Pattern. Both achieve same goal — Iterator Pattern is FOUNDATION, for-each is convenience syntax.&#xa;&#xa;Q3: Can we have multiple Iterators for same collection?&#xa;A3: YES! Multiple simultaneous iterators. Example: (1) ForwardIterator (index 0 → n), (2) ReverseIterator (index n → 0), (3) FilteredIterator (only even elements), (4) SkipIterator (every 2nd element). Each iterator maintains OWN state (index, filters). Same collection, different traversal strategies. Client chooses: library.createForwardIterator() or library.createReverseIterator().&#xa;&#xa;Q4: Fail-fast vs Fail-safe Iterators in Java?&#xa;A4: FAIL-FAST: Throws ConcurrentModificationException if collection modified during iteration (ArrayList, HashMap iterators). Detects structural modification (add/remove) via modCount. FAIL-SAFE: Works on COPY of collection, doesn't throw exception (CopyOnWriteArrayList, ConcurrentHashMap iterators). Trade-off: fail-fast = performance (no copy), fail-safe = thread-safety (copy overhead).&#xa;&#xa;Q5: Iterator Pattern performance overhead?&#xa;A5: Minimal. One Iterator object per traversal + current position tracking. Modern JVMs optimize iterator allocation. GAINS: (1) Abstraction (client independent of data structure), (2) Multiple concurrent iterators, (3) Lazy evaluation (next() on demand), (4) Consistent API across collections. Trade-off: tiny object creation cost vs massive flexibility &amp; abstraction. For-each compiles to same bytecode as Iterator!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1810" width="1520" height="460" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
